# Shell scripts, the generated commit-msg hook, and the Python tools must be stored with
# LF in the repository regardless of any contributor's core.autocrlf setting. Windows
# checkouts tolerate CRLF, but a CRLF shebang fails outright on Linux and macOS, and this
# kit is meant to run on all three from the same checkout.
*.sh        text eol=lf
commit-msg  text eol=lf
*.py        text eol=lf
*.sql       text eol=lf

# Markdown for a different reason: none of it would break on CRLF, but the kit READS it as
# data. Agent instructions carry the finding vocabulary inline and a test compares that
# against `kit-finding.sh --vocab`; the profile template is copied verbatim into every repo
# that adopts the kit. Both compared a CRLF working tree against an LF expectation and
# reported drift that did not exist. Being read by a tool is its own reason to pin.
*.md        text eol=lf

# append-only shared log: take both sides rather than conflicting. Pinned to LF for the same
# reason as *.md above -- it is READ as data, and the indexer stores whole lines of it as
# event.payload, so a CRLF checkout would carry the CR into the row.
.project/events.ndjson merge=union text eol=lf
